[
  {
    "topicTitle": "Testing Fundamentals",
    "sections": [
      {
        "id": "stage-5-topic-1-section-1",
        "title": "Testing Fundamentals — Purpose and Role of Software Testing",
        "content": "Software testing is the disciplined process of evaluating an application to determine whether its behavior matches documented requirements and real user expectations. Testing is not limited to finding defects after development. It provides continuous evidence that individual components, integrated services, business workflows, and release artifacts behave correctly under defined conditions. In a Maven and Spring Boot delivery pipeline, tests can execute automatically whenever code is committed or a pull request is opened. A useful test describes the expected behavior, supplies controlled input, executes the relevant application logic, and verifies the observed result. Testing reduces the risk of delivering regressions, incorrect calculations, broken integrations, security weaknesses, and operational failures. It cannot prove that software has no defects; it increases confidence by examining selected scenarios systematically. Teams should connect every important test to a requirement, business rule, or known failure mode so that the test suite remains meaningful rather than becoming a collection of arbitrary assertions."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-5-topic-1-section-2",
        "title": "Testing Fundamentals — Verification, Validation, and Quality Objectives",
        "content": "Verification and validation are related but different quality activities. Verification asks whether the software was built according to its specifications, design, and implementation rules. Examples include checking method behavior against an interface contract, validating configuration structure, and confirming that a component follows an agreed technical design. Validation asks whether the delivered system solves the actual user or business problem. A service can pass every narrow unit test and still fail validation if its workflow does not meet customer expectations. A practical test strategy combines both perspectives. Verification tests should cover defined rules, boundary conditions, error handling, and technical contracts. Validation should exercise realistic user journeys and business outcomes. Quality objectives should be measurable: expected results, acceptable response behavior, supported input ranges, error conditions, and release-blocking failures must be explicit. In CI, these objectives become automated checks that provide repeatable evidence before a change is accepted or deployed."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-5-topic-1-section-3",
        "title": "Testing Fundamentals — Test Levels and Their Responsibilities",
        "content": "Software tests are commonly organized into levels according to the scope of behavior being evaluated. Unit tests examine a small unit such as a method or class with collaborators controlled where appropriate. Component tests validate a larger application component and its internal interactions. Integration tests verify communication across boundaries such as application code and a database, message broker, filesystem, or external service. System tests evaluate the assembled application as a whole, while acceptance tests assess whether business workflows satisfy agreed acceptance criteria. These levels answer different questions and should not be treated as interchangeable. A unit test that mocks a repository cannot prove that the real database schema is compatible. An integration test that starts a database does not automatically prove that every user workflow is correct. Assign each test to the level that can provide reliable evidence with the least unnecessary complexity, and make the level visible through naming, test configuration, and CI execution."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-5-topic-1-section-4",
        "title": "Testing Fundamentals — The Test Pyramid and Feedback Speed",
        "content": "The test pyramid is a planning model that encourages a broad base of fast, focused tests and a smaller number of slower, broader tests. Unit tests usually form the base because they execute quickly and isolate defects close to the code that caused them. Integration tests occupy the middle because they validate real component boundaries and require additional infrastructure or setup. End-to-end and acceptance tests form a smaller upper layer because they exercise complete workflows and are often more expensive to execute and maintain. The pyramid is guidance rather than a mandatory numerical ratio. A service with complex database behavior may need substantial integration coverage, while a user interface may require more browser-level checks. The important principle is to obtain fast feedback early and reserve expensive tests for risks that narrower tests cannot evaluate. If a pipeline relies almost entirely on slow end-to-end tests, developers may wait too long to identify simple logic defects."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-5-topic-1-section-5",
        "title": "Testing Fundamentals — Test Cases, Inputs, Expected Results, and Boundaries",
        "content": "A test case should define the condition being examined, the input or initial state, the action performed, and the expected observable result. Good test cases cover normal behavior as well as invalid input, missing values, empty collections, minimum and maximum boundaries, and relevant combinations of conditions. For example, a service that calculates a discount should be tested with an eligible customer, an ineligible customer, a zero-value order, a threshold boundary, and an invalid amount. Expected results should be explicit rather than calculated using the same production logic being tested, because repeating the implementation can reproduce the same defect in the test. Test names should describe behavior and condition so failures are understandable in CI reports. A test suite should also avoid redundant cases that exercise identical behavior without adding confidence. Review test cases whenever requirements or business rules change, and retain regression cases for defects that previously escaped into production."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-5-topic-1-section-6",
        "title": "Testing Fundamentals — Test Isolation, Independence, and Repeatability",
        "content": "A reliable test should produce the same result when it runs alone, in a different order, or as part of the complete suite. Test isolation prevents one test from depending on data, static state, environment variables, or execution side effects left by another test. Each test should establish the state it needs and clean up resources it creates. Shared databases require controlled records, unique identifiers, transaction strategies, or dedicated schemas so concurrent runs do not interfere with one another. Tests should not depend on a developer-specific directory, an undocumented local service, or a manually prepared database. Repeatability also requires controlling time, randomness, network responses, and external dependencies where those factors are not the subject of the test. In CI, parallel execution can expose hidden shared-state assumptions. When a test passes locally but fails intermittently on a build agent, investigate isolation and environmental dependencies before simply rerunning the pipeline."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-5-topic-1-section-7",
        "title": "Testing Fundamentals — Test Data and Environment Management",
        "content": "Test data is part of the test design and must be predictable, representative, and safe to use. A test should create the minimum data needed for its scenario, use clear identifiers, and avoid relying on records that happen to exist in a shared environment. Synthetic data is preferable when real customer information is unnecessary because it reduces privacy and compliance risks. Environment configuration should be explicit: database connection details, active Spring profiles, service endpoints, credentials, and feature flags must be supplied through controlled test configuration rather than hard-coded secrets. Integration tests may use disposable databases or containers to make infrastructure behavior reproducible. Test setup should fail clearly when a required dependency is unavailable instead of silently skipping important checks. Cleanup must remove temporary resources even when an assertion fails. These practices help developers reproduce failures locally and allow CI agents to execute the same test scenarios consistently."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-5-topic-1-section-8",
        "title": "Testing Fundamentals — Defect Detection, Regression, and Risk-Based Testing",
        "content": "A defect is a mismatch between actual and expected behavior. When a defect is discovered, the team should identify the affected requirement, determine the conditions that trigger the problem, and create a regression test that fails before the correction and passes after it. Regression testing protects previously working behavior when code, dependencies, configuration, or infrastructure changes. Risk-based testing helps prioritize effort by considering the likelihood of failure and its potential impact. Authentication, authorization, payment calculations, data persistence, migration logic, and release-critical workflows generally deserve stronger coverage than low-impact cosmetic behavior. A test suite should not pursue coverage numbers without considering business risk. A high percentage of executed lines does not guarantee that important outcomes or failure paths are checked. Combine requirement-based cases, defect history, code changes, and production incident patterns to decide where additional tests provide the greatest reduction in release risk."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-5-topic-1-section-9",
        "title": "Testing Fundamentals — Assertions and Meaningful Failure Messages",
        "content": "Assertions compare actual behavior with an expected condition and determine whether a test passes or fails. Useful assertions verify outcomes that matter to the behavior under test, such as returned values, state changes, emitted events, persisted records, or expected exceptions. A test that only checks that a method did not throw an exception may provide insufficient evidence when the method is expected to update important business state. Assertions should be specific enough to explain what was wrong. Prefer checking meaningful fields and conditions over comparing unrelated implementation details that change frequently. Exception tests should verify the expected exception type and, when relevant, important message or error details. Avoid excessive assertions that combine many unrelated behaviors into one test, because a single failure can obscure the actual problem. Clear assertion messages and descriptive test names make Maven Surefire and Failsafe reports easier to diagnose during automated builds."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-5-topic-1-section-10",
        "title": "Testing Fundamentals — Test Automation in a Maven CI Pipeline",
        "content": "Automated tests become part of the delivery process when the build system executes them consistently and reports their results. In a Maven project, the test phase commonly runs unit tests through the Maven Surefire Plugin, while integration tests can be bound to the integration-test and verify phases through the Maven Failsafe Plugin. A typical CI job checks out the source, selects the required JDK, resolves dependencies, compiles the project, executes tests, packages the application, and archives reports and artifacts. A failed test should normally produce a non-zero build result so that a broken change cannot pass silently. Teams should retain test reports even when a job fails, because the reports provide evidence for diagnosis. Build agents should use controlled tool versions and configuration. Do not hide failures with options that ignore test errors in a release pipeline. Separate fast pull-request feedback from broader scheduled or release validation when execution cost requires it."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-5-topic-1-section-11",
        "title": "Testing Fundamentals — Coverage Metrics and Their Limitations",
        "content": "Code coverage measures which portions of source code were executed while tests ran. Common measures include line coverage, instruction coverage, branch coverage, and method coverage. These metrics can reveal unexecuted areas and help teams identify code that may need additional tests. However, execution is not the same as verification: a test can execute a line without asserting that its result is correct. Branch coverage can show whether alternative decision paths were exercised, but it does not prove that every meaningful input combination has been tested. Coverage thresholds are most useful when applied consistently and interpreted alongside risk, test quality, and defect trends. JaCoCo is commonly used in Maven projects to collect coverage data and generate reports. Teams should examine uncovered critical logic, error handling, and boundary conditions rather than trying to increase a percentage through meaningless tests. Coverage should support engineering judgment, not replace it."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-5-topic-1-section-12",
        "title": "Testing Fundamentals — Designing a Sustainable Test Strategy",
        "content": "A sustainable test strategy defines what must be tested, at which level, with which tools, and at what point in the delivery lifecycle. Start by identifying business-critical workflows, external dependencies, data boundaries, operational risks, and failure history. Map these risks to unit, component, integration, system, and acceptance tests. Define ownership for test data, environment setup, test maintenance, and failure investigation. Establish clear rules for pull-request checks, main-branch validation, scheduled regression suites, and release gates. Keep fast feedback close to development while ensuring that broader tests execute before production release. Review flaky tests, obsolete scenarios, slow setup, and duplicate coverage regularly. A test that fails intermittently should be investigated and corrected rather than permanently ignored. The strategy should evolve as the application architecture changes, new services are introduced, and production incidents reveal missing coverage. Its goal is dependable release evidence, not simply a large number of test files."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "topicTitle": "JUnit 5 Architecture",
    "sections": [
      {
        "id": "stage-5-topic-2-section-1",
        "title": "JUnit 5 Architecture — Testing Strategy and Fundamentals",
        "content": "JUnit 5 is not a single testing library. It is a modular testing ecosystem designed to separate the programming model used by test authors from the infrastructure that discovers and executes tests. Its architecture is commonly understood through three major components: JUnit Platform, JUnit Jupiter, and JUnit Vintage. The Platform provides the foundation for launching test engines and integrating with build tools and IDEs. Jupiter provides the modern programming model, including annotations, assertions, lifecycle callbacks, and extension support. Vintage allows older JUnit 3 and JUnit 4 tests to execute on the Platform during migration. Understanding this separation helps teams diagnose why a test is not discovered, why an engine is missing, or why a build tool cannot execute a particular test style. In a production project, developers should select dependencies deliberately instead of treating every JUnit artifact as interchangeable."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-5-topic-2-section-2",
        "title": "JUnit 5 Architecture — Framework Architecture",
        "content": "The JUnit Platform is the execution foundation that connects test engines with tools such as Maven, Gradle, and IDE test runners. It defines a common mechanism for discovering tests, selecting execution requests, launching tests, and reporting execution results. The Platform does not itself define the complete test programming model used by application developers. Instead, it delegates framework-specific behavior to registered TestEngine implementations. This design allows different testing frameworks to participate in a common execution environment. Platform components include the launcher API, test engine API, and supporting reporting infrastructure. In a Maven build, the test provider and Platform-related dependencies must be compatible so that Surefire can discover and launch the intended engine. When tests compile but are not executed, investigate the Platform integration and engine availability before changing application code."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-5-topic-2-section-3",
        "title": "JUnit 5 Architecture — Test Design and Implementation",
        "content": "JUnit Jupiter is the modern JUnit programming model used to write and execute tests with annotations such as @Test, @BeforeEach, @AfterEach, @BeforeAll, and @AfterAll. Its architecture includes the Jupiter API, which developers use in source code, and the Jupiter Engine, which executes those tests through the JUnit Platform. The API provides annotations, assertions, assumptions, and extension-related contracts. The engine interprets the test model, manages lifecycle callbacks, resolves supported parameters, and reports execution outcomes to the Platform. Keeping the API and engine responsibilities separate makes the framework extensible while maintaining a clear authoring experience. A project that includes only the API may compile test classes but fail to execute them if the engine is absent from the runtime test classpath. Verify both compile-time and execution-time dependencies when diagnosing this situation."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-5-topic-2-section-4",
        "title": "JUnit 5 Architecture — Assertions and Lifecycle",
        "content": "JUnit Vintage is a TestEngine that allows JUnit 3 and JUnit 4 tests to run through the JUnit Platform. It is useful when an application has a large existing test suite and the team wants to adopt JUnit 5 incrementally rather than rewrite every test immediately. Vintage does not convert old tests into Jupiter tests; it provides compatibility execution for supported legacy test styles. A migration plan should identify which modules still require Vintage, which tests have already moved to Jupiter, and when the compatibility dependency can be removed. Retaining Vintage indefinitely without tracking legacy usage can hide migration progress and introduce unnecessary dependencies. During troubleshooting, confirm that the Vintage engine is present only when legacy tests need it and that the build tool is using a compatible Platform provider."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-5-topic-2-section-5",
        "title": "JUnit 5 Architecture — Mocking and Dependency Isolation",
        "content": "A TestEngine is the Platform extension point responsible for discovering and executing tests belonging to a particular testing framework. Jupiter and Vintage are examples of engines, but the Platform is designed so additional engines can participate without changing the central launcher architecture. An engine identifies its unique ID, discovers a test tree from an execution request, and executes selected test descriptors while reporting events. This separation means the Platform can coordinate execution while each engine understands its own test model. In enterprise builds, engine selection matters when multiple testing frameworks or legacy suites coexist. If a test is not discovered, inspect the engine dependencies, engine identifiers, classpath, and discovery filters. Do not assume that the presence of the JUnit API automatically registers an execution engine."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-5-topic-2-section-6",
        "title": "JUnit 5 Architecture — Unit and Integration Testing",
        "content": "The TestEngine discovery and execution model explains how a build tool turns compiled test classes into executed test cases. A launcher creates a discovery request containing selectors and filters, such as package, class, method, or tag selections. Registered engines inspect that request and return test descriptors representing the discovered test hierarchy. The launcher then coordinates execution and receives events such as test started, test successful, test failed, or test skipped. This model supports consistent reporting across different engines and execution environments. A class can compile successfully but remain absent from the execution report because its name does not match discovery patterns, its annotations are unsupported, or the relevant engine is missing. When investigating missing tests, compare the requested selectors, engine discovery output, naming conventions, and actual test runtime classpath."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-5-topic-2-section-7",
        "title": "JUnit 5 Architecture — Container-Based Testing",
        "content": "The JUnit Platform Launcher API provides a programmatic interface for discovering and executing tests outside the direct control of a conventional build command. It accepts a LauncherDiscoveryRequest, discovers tests through available engines, and executes the resulting test plan. Execution listeners can observe test-plan and test-case events, enabling custom reporting, integrations, or specialized test orchestration. Most application teams do not need to write a custom launcher because Maven Surefire, Gradle, and IDE integrations already provide this functionality. However, understanding the launcher is valuable when building internal test tools or diagnosing differences between IDE and CI execution. Custom launcher code should avoid duplicating build-tool responsibilities and should use compatible Platform versions. Test selection, listener behavior, and engine registration must be explicit for predictable results."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-5-topic-2-section-8",
        "title": "JUnit 5 Architecture — Coverage and Quality Measurement",
        "content": "JUnit test descriptors form a hierarchical representation of the discovered test plan. A descriptor can represent an engine, test class, nested test container, or individual test case. The Platform uses this structure to organize discovery and execution events consistently, while the specific engine determines how its descriptors map to framework constructs. This hierarchy supports reporting at multiple levels, including containers and leaf tests. It also helps tools display nested tests and aggregate outcomes. When a report shows a container failure without an obvious failed method, inspect the full test tree and lifecycle events because failures can occur during class setup, extension callbacks, or container execution. Understanding descriptors is especially useful when interpreting CI reports and custom listener output rather than relying only on the final test count."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-5-topic-2-section-9",
        "title": "JUnit 5 Architecture — Test Reporting",
        "content": "JUnit 5 extensions provide a controlled mechanism for integrating reusable behavior into the Jupiter test lifecycle. Extensions can participate in test instance creation, parameter resolution, lifecycle callbacks, exception handling, and conditional test execution. They replace many use cases previously implemented with JUnit 4 runners and rules, while supporting composition through a common extension model. Teams can register extensions declaratively with annotations or programmatically where appropriate. Common examples include dependency injection, temporary resource management, test reporting, and custom environment conditions. Extensions should have a clearly defined responsibility and predictable lifecycle behavior. Avoid placing unrelated application business logic inside a test extension. When an extension causes unexpected test failures, inspect its registration scope, callback ordering, parameter resolution, and exception handling behavior."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-5-topic-2-section-10",
        "title": "JUnit 5 Architecture — Flaky Test Investigation",
        "content": "JUnit 5 parameter resolution allows supported values to be supplied to test constructors, lifecycle methods, and test methods. Jupiter includes built-in resolvers for framework-managed parameters and supports custom ParameterResolver extensions for project-specific needs. A resolver decides whether it supports a parameter and, when selected, provides the corresponding value. This mechanism enables clean integration with test context objects, shared fixtures, and specialized resources without embedding repetitive setup code in every test. Custom resolvers should validate supported parameter types carefully and fail with clear messages when configuration is missing. Multiple resolvers may participate in a test, so teams should avoid ambiguous resolution rules. When a parameterized test or lifecycle method fails before the test body runs, investigate resolver registration and parameter support as well as the test method signature."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-5-topic-2-section-11",
        "title": "JUnit 5 Architecture — CI Integration",
        "content": "JUnit 5 lifecycle execution defines when test instances are created and when setup and cleanup callbacks run. Jupiter supports per-method and per-class test instance lifecycles, with per-method behavior commonly providing stronger isolation because a new test instance is created for each test method. Lifecycle annotations control callbacks around individual tests and test classes. Their ordering and interaction with nested tests, extensions, and inherited methods can affect resource management. A reliable test should release files, connections, containers, and other external resources even when assertions fail. Shared mutable state can make results depend on execution order and can create failures when parallel execution is enabled. Choose lifecycle behavior intentionally, document shared fixtures, and prefer explicit cleanup mechanisms that remain safe when tests are skipped or interrupted."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-5-topic-2-section-12",
        "title": "JUnit 5 Architecture — Production Test Strategy",
        "content": "JUnit Platform reporting communicates discovery and execution outcomes to the calling build tool or IDE. Test execution generates events for test plans, containers, and individual tests, allowing integrations to create console output, XML reports, IDE views, and CI summaries. The reporting model distinguishes successful, failed, aborted, and skipped execution outcomes. A test may be discovered but not executed because of filters, assumptions, or conditional execution. A build may also fail during discovery or engine initialization before ordinary test events are produced. When a CI report appears incomplete, compare the build exit code, Surefire or Failsafe reports, test counts, and console diagnostics. Do not interpret a missing report as proof that all tests passed. Reliable pipelines preserve test reports as build artifacts so failures can be investigated after the job ends."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-5-topic-2-section-13",
        "title": "JUnit 5 Architecture — Hands-on Testing Exercise",
        "content": "JUnit 5 integrates with build tools and IDEs through Platform-aware test execution providers. In Maven projects, Surefire is commonly responsible for unit-test execution, while Failsafe is commonly used for integration-test execution in designated lifecycle phases. The provider must be compatible with the JUnit Platform version and available engines. IDEs may use their own launch integrations, so a test that runs locally from an IDE may behave differently in a clean CI environment if dependencies or discovery configuration differ. Standardize the JDK, Maven version, dependency versions, and test naming conventions across developer and CI environments. When tests compile but are not executed, inspect the effective POM, plugin version, provider selection, test classpath, and engine dependencies before modifying test source code."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-5-topic-2-section-14",
        "title": "JUnit 5 Architecture — Testing Strategy and Fundamentals — JUnit 5 Architecture [stage-5-topic-2-section-14]",
        "content": "JUnit 5 supports migration from older JUnit versions through a staged approach. Existing JUnit 3 and JUnit 4 tests can remain available through the Vintage engine while new tests are written with Jupiter. Migration work should inventory legacy annotations, runners, rules, lifecycle methods, assertions, and custom test infrastructure. Some concepts have direct replacements, while others require redesign using Jupiter extensions or different test organization. Migrate a representative module first and verify that both old and new tests execute in the same build. Track legacy test counts so the team can measure progress and eventually remove compatibility dependencies. Avoid changing annotations mechanically without validating lifecycle behavior, assertion semantics, and extension interactions. A successful migration preserves test intent and confidence rather than merely producing a compiling test suite.\r\n\r\nA production JUnit 5 architecture should be reproducible, observable, and maintainable across local development and continuous integration. Pin compatible versions through dependency management, include the required Jupiter engine, and configure a Platform-compatible test provider in the build plugin. Keep test APIs scoped to test compilation and avoid introducing test libraries into the production runtime artifact. Separate unit and integration test responsibilities, retain machine-readable reports, and document any Vintage compatibility requirement. Validate the architecture with a clean command-line build rather than relying only on IDE execution. When failures occur, classify them as dependency resolution, engine discovery, test selection, lifecycle, extension, or test-code failures. This classification makes troubleshooting faster and prevents teams from changing unrelated application code to solve build infrastructure problems."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "topicTitle": "Test Annotations",
    "sections": [
      {
        "id": "stage-5-topic-3-section-1",
        "title": "Test Annotations — Testing Strategy and Fundamentals",
        "content": "# Test Annotations — Testing Strategy and Fundamentals\n\n## 1. Explanation and Learning Objective\n\nThis section belongs to **Test Annotations** in Stage 05: Kubernetes Container Orchestration.\n\nKubernetes manages containerized applications through a declarative API and a set of controllers that continuously reconcile actual cluster state with the desired state declared in resource specifications. The relevant components may include Pods, workload controllers, Services, networking, storage, configuration resources, scheduling, security controls, and observability systems. The engineering objective is to run applications reliably, securely, and predictably across a cluster.\n\nDefine the Kubernetes concept represented by this section and explain the problem it solves. Describe where it fits in a cluster and application lifecycle. Identify the relevant API resource, controller, node component, or external integration. Explain technical terms in beginner-friendly language and distinguish Kubernetes orchestration from the responsibility of the application running inside a container.\n\nAfter completing this section, an engineer should be able to explain the concept in plain English, identify the Kubernetes resources and components involved, perform the relevant operation, inspect cluster state, and recognize the risks of an incorrect implementation.\n\n## 2. Kubernetes Technical Architecture and Behavior\n\nKubernetes uses a declarative operating model. An engineer declares the desired state in API resources, and Kubernetes controllers continuously work to reconcile the actual cluster state with that desired state.\n\nThe API server is the main entry point for cluster operations. Authentication, authorization, and admission controls determine whether a request can be accepted. etcd stores cluster state. Controllers monitor resources and create or update dependent objects. The scheduler assigns unscheduled Pods to suitable nodes. The kubelet on each worker node works with the container runtime to ensure assigned workloads run as specified.\n\nFor **Test Annotations — Testing Strategy and Fundamentals**, identify the API resource, controller, node component, or external integration responsible for the behavior. Explain what state is submitted, what reconciliation takes place, and how the result can be observed. Do not assume that a successful kubectl apply means the application is healthy. API acceptance, scheduling, container startup, readiness, and application availability are separate conditions.\n\n## 3. Implementation Workflow\n\n1. Confirm the current Kubernetes context with kubectl config current-context.\n2. Select and verify the correct namespace.\n3. Inspect the relevant resources before changing anything.\n4. Review the manifest fields, image reference, labels, selectors, resources, and permissions.\n5. Apply the required configuration using a reviewed manifest or approved deployment process.\n6. Observe controller reconciliation and Pod scheduling.\n7. Inspect Pod conditions, container state, events, and logs.\n8. Verify networking, storage, and application readiness where required.\n9. Record the manifest revision, image digest, and resulting workload state.\n\nUse namespaces and explicit resource names to avoid changing the wrong environment. Before deleting or replacing resources, determine whether they own persistent data or production traffic.\n\n## 4. Practical Kubernetes Commands\n\nThe following commands provide relevant inspection and operational examples. Replace placeholder resource names and namespaces with actual values.\n\n`\bash id=\"k8s501\"\nkubectl cluster-info\nkubectl get nodes -o wide\nkubectl get namespaces\nkubectl get pods -A\nkubectl get services -A\nkubectl get events -A --sort-by=.metadata.creationTimestamp\n`\n\nCommands that modify resources should be executed only after checking the active context and reviewing the target manifest. Avoid copying production credentials or secret values into terminal history.\n\n## 5. Kubernetes Workload Example\n\nThe following Deployment demonstrates a basic stateless application workload:\n\n`yaml id=\"k8s502\"\napiVersion: apps/v1\nkind: Deployment\nmetadata:\n  name: payments-api\n  namespace: production\nspec:\n  replicas: 3\n  selector:\n    matchLabels:\n      app: payments-api\n  template:\n    metadata:\n      labels:\n        app: payments-api\n    spec:\n      containers:\n        - name: application\n          image: registry.example.com/payments-api:1.4.0\n          ports:\n            - name: http\n              containerPort: 8080\n          resources:\n            requests:\n              cpu: \"250m\"\n              memory: \"256Mi\"\n            limits:\n              cpu: \"1000m\"\n              memory: \"512Mi\"\n          readinessProbe:\n            httpGet:\n              path: /health\n              port: http\n            initialDelaySeconds: 10\n            periodSeconds: 5\n          livenessProbe:\n            httpGet:\n              path: /health\n              port: http\n            initialDelaySeconds: 30\n            periodSeconds: 10\n`\n\nThe Deployment declares three desired replicas. Its controller creates and maintains the Pods. Resource requests help the scheduler select suitable nodes, while limits constrain container consumption. Readiness determines whether a Pod should receive Service traffic. The example must be adapted to the actual application health endpoints and resource profile.\n\n## 6. Production DevOps Scenario\n\nConsider a Java payment application deployed to a Kubernetes cluster. The development team stores its source code and Kubernetes manifests in version control. A CI pipeline builds and tests the application, scans the image, and publishes an immutable image digest to a registry.\n\nA delivery process updates the Deployment manifest to reference the approved image. Kubernetes accepts the updated desired state and begins a rolling update. The scheduler selects nodes with sufficient available resources. Kubelets start the containers, and readiness probes determine when the new Pods can receive traffic.\n\nThe Service routes requests to ready endpoints. Monitoring records application latency, error rates, Pod restarts, CPU usage, memory usage, and node health. If the new version fails readiness or application validation, the delivery process stops or rolls back according to the deployment policy.\n\nThe team records the source commit, image digest, manifest revision, deployment time, and validation result. This makes it possible to identify exactly which application version is running during an incident.\n\n## 7. Common Mistakes and Their Impact\n\n- Applying manifests to the wrong Kubernetes context can change an unintended cluster.\n- Incorrect labels or selectors can leave Services without matching Pod endpoints.\n- Missing resource requests can make scheduling unpredictable under resource pressure.\n- Excessively low memory limits can cause containers to be terminated with OOMKilled.\n- Incorrect readiness probes can prevent healthy applications from receiving traffic.\n- Incorrect liveness probes can repeatedly restart an application that is still recovering.\n- Using mutable image tags weakens deployment traceability.\n- Broad RBAC permissions increase the impact of compromised identities.\n- Deleting PersistentVolumeClaims without understanding reclaim behavior can cause data loss.\n- Assuming Pod creation means application readiness can cause outages.\n- Ignoring namespace boundaries can lead to incorrect resource inspection or modification.\n\n## 8. Troubleshooting and Safe Recovery\n\nStart by confirming the active context, namespace, resource name, and timestamp of the failure. Inspect resource state using kubectl get and kubectl describe. Review events for scheduling failures, image-pull errors, mount failures, probe failures, and node pressure.\n\nFor a Pending Pod, inspect scheduling events, node capacity, resource requests, taints, tolerations, and affinity. For ImagePullBackOff, verify the image reference, registry availability, and image-pull credentials. For CrashLoopBackOff, inspect current and previous container logs, exit codes, commands, configuration, and resource limits.\n\nFor service connectivity problems, inspect Service selectors, EndpointSlices, DNS, network policies, and the cluster network implementation. For storage failures, inspect PVC binding, PV state, CSI components, access modes, and mount events.\n\nApply the smallest safe correction. Preserve evidence before deleting Pods or changing shared resources. Verify the recovery through resource conditions and an actual application request.\n\n## 9. Security and Production Best Practices\n\nUse least-privilege RBAC for human users and service accounts. Avoid granting cluster-admin access to application workloads. Protect Secrets through encryption at rest, restrictive access, controlled rotation, and secure external secret integration where required.\n\nUse trusted and scanned container images. Run containers as non-root where possible, drop unnecessary Linux capabilities, restrict privilege escalation, and use appropriate seccomp and Pod security controls. Apply network policies when supported by the cluster network plugin.\n\nSet realistic resource requests and limits. Configure readiness, liveness, and startup probes according to application behavior. Use controlled rollout strategies, topology-aware scheduling, disruption budgets, and capacity planning for important services.\n\nBack up stateful data and test restoration. Monitor control-plane access, node health, Pod restarts, application SLOs, storage capacity, and cluster resource pressure. Keep cluster versions, add-ons, and workload manifests within supported compatibility ranges.\n\n## 10. Verification and Completion Criteria\n\nThe operation represented by **Test Annotations — Testing Strategy and Fundamentals** is complete only when the declared and actual cluster states are consistent and the application behavior has been verified.\n\nCheck that:\n\n- The intended cluster context and namespace were used.\n- The correct API resources were created or updated.\n- Selectors and labels connect the intended resources.\n- Pods are scheduled and containers start successfully.\n- Readiness and health checks report the expected state.\n- Services expose the intended endpoints.\n- Required storage is mounted and persistent data is safe.\n- Resource requests, limits, and security settings match policy.\n- Events and logs contain no unresolved operational failures.\n- The application responds correctly through its intended access path.\n- The image and manifest revisions can be traced to the approved release.\n\n## 11. Practical Exercise\n\nCreate a namespace and deploy a small application using a Deployment manifest. Add a Service that selects the application Pods. Inspect the Deployment, ReplicaSet, Pods, Service, and endpoints.\n\nChange the image reference to an invalid image and observe the resulting image-pull failure. Use kubectl describe and events to identify the reason. Restore the valid image reference and observe the successful rollout.\n\nTest the application through the Service. Explain the difference between a Pod being created, a container running, a Pod becoming ready, and the application successfully responding to a request.\n\n## 12. Interview and Engineering Understanding\n\nAn engineer should be able to explain what **Test Annotations — Testing Strategy and Fundamentals** means, why Kubernetes provides the relevant capability, which API resources and cluster components participate, how reconciliation or execution works, and how the final state is verified.\n\nA strong practical answer describes the desired state, the actual state before and after the operation, the relevant manifest fields or commands, the evidence collected, and the production risks. Avoid simply stating that kubectl apply was successful. Explain whether the workload was scheduled, started, became ready, and served real application traffic."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-5-topic-3-section-2",
        "title": "Test Annotations — Framework Architecture",
        "content": "# Test Annotations — Framework Architecture\n\n## 1. Explanation and Learning Objective\n\nThis section belongs to **Test Annotations** in Stage 05: Kubernetes Container Orchestration.\n\nKubernetes manages containerized applications through a declarative API and a set of controllers that continuously reconcile actual cluster state with the desired state declared in resource specifications. The relevant components may include Pods, workload controllers, Services, networking, storage, configuration resources, scheduling, security controls, and observability systems. The engineering objective is to run applications reliably, securely, and predictably across a cluster.\n\nExplain the architecture and responsibilities of the components involved. Describe API requests, authentication and authorization, admission processing, persistence in etcd, controller reconciliation, scheduling, kubelet coordination, and container-runtime interaction where relevant. Identify which component makes the decision, which component executes the work, and how the resulting state becomes visible through the Kubernetes API. Explain the impact of control-plane or node-component failures.\n\nAfter completing this section, an engineer should be able to explain the concept in plain English, identify the Kubernetes resources and components involved, perform the relevant operation, inspect cluster state, and recognize the risks of an incorrect implementation.\n\n## 2. Kubernetes Technical Architecture and Behavior\n\nKubernetes uses a declarative operating model. An engineer declares the desired state in API resources, and Kubernetes controllers continuously work to reconcile the actual cluster state with that desired state.\n\nThe API server is the main entry point for cluster operations. Authentication, authorization, and admission controls determine whether a request can be accepted. etcd stores cluster state. Controllers monitor resources and create or update dependent objects. The scheduler assigns unscheduled Pods to suitable nodes. The kubelet on each worker node works with the container runtime to ensure assigned workloads run as specified.\n\nFor **Test Annotations — Framework Architecture**, identify the API resource, controller, node component, or external integration responsible for the behavior. Explain what state is submitted, what reconciliation takes place, and how the result can be observed. Do not assume that a successful kubectl apply means the application is healthy. API acceptance, scheduling, container startup, readiness, and application availability are separate conditions.\n\n## 3. Implementation Workflow\n\n1. Confirm the current Kubernetes context with kubectl config current-context.\n2. Select and verify the correct namespace.\n3. Inspect the relevant resources before changing anything.\n4. Review the manifest fields, image reference, labels, selectors, resources, and permissions.\n5. Apply the required configuration using a reviewed manifest or approved deployment process.\n6. Observe controller reconciliation and Pod scheduling.\n7. Inspect Pod conditions, container state, events, and logs.\n8. Verify networking, storage, and application readiness where required.\n9. Record the manifest revision, image digest, and resulting workload state.\n\nUse namespaces and explicit resource names to avoid changing the wrong environment. Before deleting or replacing resources, determine whether they own persistent data or production traffic.\n\n## 4. Practical Kubernetes Commands\n\nThe following commands provide relevant inspection and operational examples. Replace placeholder resource names and namespaces with actual values.\n\n`\bash id=\"k8s501\"\nkubectl cluster-info\nkubectl get nodes -o wide\nkubectl get namespaces\nkubectl get pods -A\nkubectl get services -A\nkubectl get events -A --sort-by=.metadata.creationTimestamp\n`\n\nCommands that modify resources should be executed only after checking the active context and reviewing the target manifest. Avoid copying production credentials or secret values into terminal history.\n\n## 5. Kubernetes Workload Example\n\nThe following Deployment demonstrates a basic stateless application workload:\n\n`yaml id=\"k8s502\"\napiVersion: apps/v1\nkind: Deployment\nmetadata:\n  name: payments-api\n  namespace: production\nspec:\n  replicas: 3\n  selector:\n    matchLabels:\n      app: payments-api\n  template:\n    metadata:\n      labels:\n        app: payments-api\n    spec:\n      containers:\n        - name: application\n          image: registry.example.com/payments-api:1.4.0\n          ports:\n            - name: http\n              containerPort: 8080\n          resources:\n            requests:\n              cpu: \"250m\"\n              memory: \"256Mi\"\n            limits:\n              cpu: \"1000m\"\n              memory: \"512Mi\"\n          readinessProbe:\n            httpGet:\n              path: /health\n              port: http\n            initialDelaySeconds: 10\n            periodSeconds: 5\n          livenessProbe:\n            httpGet:\n              path: /health\n              port: http\n            initialDelaySeconds: 30\n            periodSeconds: 10\n`\n\nThe Deployment declares three desired replicas. Its controller creates and maintains the Pods. Resource requests help the scheduler select suitable nodes, while limits constrain container consumption. Readiness determines whether a Pod should receive Service traffic. The example must be adapted to the actual application health endpoints and resource profile.\n\n## 6. Production DevOps Scenario\n\nConsider a Java payment application deployed to a Kubernetes cluster. The development team stores its source code and Kubernetes manifests in version control. A CI pipeline builds and tests the application, scans the image, and publishes an immutable image digest to a registry.\n\nA delivery process updates the Deployment manifest to reference the approved image. Kubernetes accepts the updated desired state and begins a rolling update. The scheduler selects nodes with sufficient available resources. Kubelets start the containers, and readiness probes determine when the new Pods can receive traffic.\n\nThe Service routes requests to ready endpoints. Monitoring records application latency, error rates, Pod restarts, CPU usage, memory usage, and node health. If the new version fails readiness or application validation, the delivery process stops or rolls back according to the deployment policy.\n\nThe team records the source commit, image digest, manifest revision, deployment time, and validation result. This makes it possible to identify exactly which application version is running during an incident.\n\n## 7. Common Mistakes and Their Impact\n\n- Applying manifests to the wrong Kubernetes context can change an unintended cluster.\n- Incorrect labels or selectors can leave Services without matching Pod endpoints.\n- Missing resource requests can make scheduling unpredictable under resource pressure.\n- Excessively low memory limits can cause containers to be terminated with OOMKilled.\n- Incorrect readiness probes can prevent healthy applications from receiving traffic.\n- Incorrect liveness probes can repeatedly restart an application that is still recovering.\n- Using mutable image tags weakens deployment traceability.\n- Broad RBAC permissions increase the impact of compromised identities.\n- Deleting PersistentVolumeClaims without understanding reclaim behavior can cause data loss.\n- Assuming Pod creation means application readiness can cause outages.\n- Ignoring namespace boundaries can lead to incorrect resource inspection or modification.\n\n## 8. Troubleshooting and Safe Recovery\n\nStart by confirming the active context, namespace, resource name, and timestamp of the failure. Inspect resource state using kubectl get and kubectl describe. Review events for scheduling failures, image-pull errors, mount failures, probe failures, and node pressure.\n\nFor a Pending Pod, inspect scheduling events, node capacity, resource requests, taints, tolerations, and affinity. For ImagePullBackOff, verify the image reference, registry availability, and image-pull credentials. For CrashLoopBackOff, inspect current and previous container logs, exit codes, commands, configuration, and resource limits.\n\nFor service connectivity problems, inspect Service selectors, EndpointSlices, DNS, network policies, and the cluster network implementation. For storage failures, inspect PVC binding, PV state, CSI components, access modes, and mount events.\n\nApply the smallest safe correction. Preserve evidence before deleting Pods or changing shared resources. Verify the recovery through resource conditions and an actual application request.\n\n## 9. Security and Production Best Practices\n\nUse least-privilege RBAC for human users and service accounts. Avoid granting cluster-admin access to application workloads. Protect Secrets through encryption at rest, restrictive access, controlled rotation, and secure external secret integration where required.\n\nUse trusted and scanned container images. Run containers as non-root where possible, drop unnecessary Linux capabilities, restrict privilege escalation, and use appropriate seccomp and Pod security controls. Apply network policies when supported by the cluster network plugin.\n\nSet realistic resource requests and limits. Configure readiness, liveness, and startup probes according to application behavior. Use controlled rollout strategies, topology-aware scheduling, disruption budgets, and capacity planning for important services.\n\nBack up stateful data and test restoration. Monitor control-plane access, node health, Pod restarts, application SLOs, storage capacity, and cluster resource pressure. Keep cluster versions, add-ons, and workload manifests within supported compatibility ranges.\n\n## 10. Verification and Completion Criteria\n\nThe operation represented by **Test Annotations — Framework Architecture** is complete only when the declared and actual cluster states are consistent and the application behavior has been verified.\n\nCheck that:\n\n- The intended cluster context and namespace were used.\n- The correct API resources were created or updated.\n- Selectors and labels connect the intended resources.\n- Pods are scheduled and containers start successfully.\n- Readiness and health checks report the expected state.\n- Services expose the intended endpoints.\n- Required storage is mounted and persistent data is safe.\n- Resource requests, limits, and security settings match policy.\n- Events and logs contain no unresolved operational failures.\n- The application responds correctly through its intended access path.\n- The image and manifest revisions can be traced to the approved release.\n\n## 11. Practical Exercise\n\nCreate a namespace and deploy a small application using a Deployment manifest. Add a Service that selects the application Pods. Inspect the Deployment, ReplicaSet, Pods, Service, and endpoints.\n\nChange the image reference to an invalid image and observe the resulting image-pull failure. Use kubectl describe and events to identify the reason. Restore the valid image reference and observe the successful rollout.\n\nTest the application through the Service. Explain the difference between a Pod being created, a container running, a Pod becoming ready, and the application successfully responding to a request.\n\n## 12. Interview and Engineering Understanding\n\nAn engineer should be able to explain what **Test Annotations — Framework Architecture** means, why Kubernetes provides the relevant capability, which API resources and cluster components participate, how reconciliation or execution works, and how the final state is verified.\n\nA strong practical answer describes the desired state, the actual state before and after the operation, the relevant manifest fields or commands, the evidence collected, and the production risks. Avoid simply stating that kubectl apply was successful. Explain whether the workload was scheduled, started, became ready, and served real application traffic."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-5-topic-3-section-3",
        "title": "Test Annotations — Test Design and Implementation",
        "content": "# Test Annotations — Test Design and Implementation\n\n## 1. Explanation and Learning Objective\n\nThis section belongs to **Test Annotations** in Stage 05: Kubernetes Container Orchestration.\n\nKubernetes manages containerized applications through a declarative API and a set of controllers that continuously reconcile actual cluster state with the desired state declared in resource specifications. The relevant components may include Pods, workload controllers, Services, networking, storage, configuration resources, scheduling, security controls, and observability systems. The engineering objective is to run applications reliably, securely, and predictably across a cluster.\n\nDescribe a reproducible Kubernetes exercise. State the cluster prerequisites, namespace, manifests, required images, resource settings, and commands. Explain the expected state after each operation and how to inspect it. Include a controlled failure, such as an invalid image, incorrect Service selector, insufficient resource request, or failing readiness probe. Verify recovery through resource status, events, logs, endpoints, and an application-level request.\n\nAfter completing this section, an engineer should be able to explain the concept in plain English, identify the Kubernetes resources and components involved, perform the relevant operation, inspect cluster state, and recognize the risks of an incorrect implementation.\n\n## 2. Kubernetes Technical Architecture and Behavior\n\nKubernetes uses a declarative operating model. An engineer declares the desired state in API resources, and Kubernetes controllers continuously work to reconcile the actual cluster state with that desired state.\n\nThe API server is the main entry point for cluster operations. Authentication, authorization, and admission controls determine whether a request can be accepted. etcd stores cluster state. Controllers monitor resources and create or update dependent objects. The scheduler assigns unscheduled Pods to suitable nodes. The kubelet on each worker node works with the container runtime to ensure assigned workloads run as specified.\n\nFor **Test Annotations — Test Design and Implementation**, identify the API resource, controller, node component, or external integration responsible for the behavior. Explain what state is submitted, what reconciliation takes place, and how the result can be observed. Do not assume that a successful kubectl apply means the application is healthy. API acceptance, scheduling, container startup, readiness, and application availability are separate conditions.\n\n## 3. Implementation Workflow\n\n1. Confirm the current Kubernetes context with kubectl config current-context.\n2. Select and verify the correct namespace.\n3. Inspect the relevant resources before changing anything.\n4. Review the manifest fields, image reference, labels, selectors, resources, and permissions.\n5. Apply the required configuration using a reviewed manifest or approved deployment process.\n6. Observe controller reconciliation and Pod scheduling.\n7. Inspect Pod conditions, container state, events, and logs.\n8. Verify networking, storage, and application readiness where required.\n9. Record the manifest revision, image digest, and resulting workload state.\n\nUse namespaces and explicit resource names to avoid changing the wrong environment. Before deleting or replacing resources, determine whether they own persistent data or production traffic.\n\n## 4. Practical Kubernetes Commands\n\nThe following commands provide relevant inspection and operational examples. Replace placeholder resource names and namespaces with actual values.\n\n`\bash id=\"k8s501\"\nkubectl cluster-info\nkubectl get nodes -o wide\nkubectl get namespaces\nkubectl get pods -A\nkubectl get services -A\nkubectl get events -A --sort-by=.metadata.creationTimestamp\n`\n\nCommands that modify resources should be executed only after checking the active context and reviewing the target manifest. Avoid copying production credentials or secret values into terminal history.\n\n## 5. Kubernetes Workload Example\n\nThe following Deployment demonstrates a basic stateless application workload:\n\n`yaml id=\"k8s502\"\napiVersion: apps/v1\nkind: Deployment\nmetadata:\n  name: payments-api\n  namespace: production\nspec:\n  replicas: 3\n  selector:\n    matchLabels:\n      app: payments-api\n  template:\n    metadata:\n      labels:\n        app: payments-api\n    spec:\n      containers:\n        - name: application\n          image: registry.example.com/payments-api:1.4.0\n          ports:\n            - name: http\n              containerPort: 8080\n          resources:\n            requests:\n              cpu: \"250m\"\n              memory: \"256Mi\"\n            limits:\n              cpu: \"1000m\"\n              memory: \"512Mi\"\n          readinessProbe:\n            httpGet:\n              path: /health\n              port: http\n            initialDelaySeconds: 10\n            periodSeconds: 5\n          livenessProbe:\n            httpGet:\n              path: /health\n              port: http\n            initialDelaySeconds: 30\n            periodSeconds: 10\n`\n\nThe Deployment declares three desired replicas. Its controller creates and maintains the Pods. Resource requests help the scheduler select suitable nodes, while limits constrain container consumption. Readiness determines whether a Pod should receive Service traffic. The example must be adapted to the actual application health endpoints and resource profile.\n\n## 6. Production DevOps Scenario\n\nConsider a Java payment application deployed to a Kubernetes cluster. The development team stores its source code and Kubernetes manifests in version control. A CI pipeline builds and tests the application, scans the image, and publishes an immutable image digest to a registry.\n\nA delivery process updates the Deployment manifest to reference the approved image. Kubernetes accepts the updated desired state and begins a rolling update. The scheduler selects nodes with sufficient available resources. Kubelets start the containers, and readiness probes determine when the new Pods can receive traffic.\n\nThe Service routes requests to ready endpoints. Monitoring records application latency, error rates, Pod restarts, CPU usage, memory usage, and node health. If the new version fails readiness or application validation, the delivery process stops or rolls back according to the deployment policy.\n\nThe team records the source commit, image digest, manifest revision, deployment time, and validation result. This makes it possible to identify exactly which application version is running during an incident.\n\n## 7. Common Mistakes and Their Impact\n\n- Applying manifests to the wrong Kubernetes context can change an unintended cluster.\n- Incorrect labels or selectors can leave Services without matching Pod endpoints.\n- Missing resource requests can make scheduling unpredictable under resource pressure.\n- Excessively low memory limits can cause containers to be terminated with OOMKilled.\n- Incorrect readiness probes can prevent healthy applications from receiving traffic.\n- Incorrect liveness probes can repeatedly restart an application that is still recovering.\n- Using mutable image tags weakens deployment traceability.\n- Broad RBAC permissions increase the impact of compromised identities.\n- Deleting PersistentVolumeClaims without understanding reclaim behavior can cause data loss.\n- Assuming Pod creation means application readiness can cause outages.\n- Ignoring namespace boundaries can lead to incorrect resource inspection or modification.\n\n## 8. Troubleshooting and Safe Recovery\n\nStart by confirming the active context, namespace, resource name, and timestamp of the failure. Inspect resource state using kubectl get and kubectl describe. Review events for scheduling failures, image-pull errors, mount failures, probe failures, and node pressure.\n\nFor a Pending Pod, inspect scheduling events, node capacity, resource requests, taints, tolerations, and affinity. For ImagePullBackOff, verify the image reference, registry availability, and image-pull credentials. For CrashLoopBackOff, inspect current and previous container logs, exit codes, commands, configuration, and resource limits.\n\nFor service connectivity problems, inspect Service selectors, EndpointSlices, DNS, network policies, and the cluster network implementation. For storage failures, inspect PVC binding, PV state, CSI components, access modes, and mount events.\n\nApply the smallest safe correction. Preserve evidence before deleting Pods or changing shared resources. Verify the recovery through resource conditions and an actual application request.\n\n## 9. Security and Production Best Practices\n\nUse least-privilege RBAC for human users and service accounts. Avoid granting cluster-admin access to application workloads. Protect Secrets through encryption at rest, restrictive access, controlled rotation, and secure external secret integration where required.\n\nUse trusted and scanned container images. Run containers as non-root where possible, drop unnecessary Linux capabilities, restrict privilege escalation, and use appropriate seccomp and Pod security controls. Apply network policies when supported by the cluster network plugin.\n\nSet realistic resource requests and limits. Configure readiness, liveness, and startup probes according to application behavior. Use controlled rollout strategies, topology-aware scheduling, disruption budgets, and capacity planning for important services.\n\nBack up stateful data and test restoration. Monitor control-plane access, node health, Pod restarts, application SLOs, storage capacity, and cluster resource pressure. Keep cluster versions, add-ons, and workload manifests within supported compatibility ranges.\n\n## 10. Verification and Completion Criteria\n\nThe operation represented by **Test Annotations — Test Design and Implementation** is complete only when the declared and actual cluster states are consistent and the application behavior has been verified.\n\nCheck that:\n\n- The intended cluster context and namespace were used.\n- The correct API resources were created or updated.\n- Selectors and labels connect the intended resources.\n- Pods are scheduled and containers start successfully.\n- Readiness and health checks report the expected state.\n- Services expose the intended endpoints.\n- Required storage is mounted and persistent data is safe.\n- Resource requests, limits, and security settings match policy.\n- Events and logs contain no unresolved operational failures.\n- The application responds correctly through its intended access path.\n- The image and manifest revisions can be traced to the approved release.\n\n## 11. Practical Exercise\n\nCreate a namespace and deploy a small application using a Deployment manifest. Add a Service that selects the application Pods. Inspect the Deployment, ReplicaSet, Pods, Service, and endpoints.\n\nChange the image reference to an invalid image and observe the resulting image-pull failure. Use kubectl describe and events to identify the reason. Restore the valid image reference and observe the successful rollout.\n\nTest the application through the Service. Explain the difference between a Pod being created, a container running, a Pod becoming ready, and the application successfully responding to a request.\n\n## 12. Interview and Engineering Understanding\n\nAn engineer should be able to explain what **Test Annotations — Test Design and Implementation** means, why Kubernetes provides the relevant capability, which API resources and cluster components participate, how reconciliation or execution works, and how the final state is verified.\n\nA strong practical answer describes the desired state, the actual state before and after the operation, the relevant manifest fields or commands, the evidence collected, and the production risks. Avoid simply stating that kubectl apply was successful. Explain whether the workload was scheduled, started, became ready, and served real application traffic."
