n represented by **Spring Boot Integration Testing — Assertions and Lifecycle** is complete only when the declared and actual cluster states are consistent and the application behavior has been verified.\n\nCheck that:\n\n- The intended cluster context and namespace were used.\n- The correct API resources were created or updated.\n- Selectors and labels connect the intended resources.\n- Pods are scheduled and containers start successfully.\n- Readiness and health checks report the expected state.\n- Services expose the intended endpoints.\n- Required storage is mounted and persistent data is safe.\n- Resource requests, limits, and security settings match policy.\n- Events and logs contain no unresolved operational failures.\n- The application responds correctly through its intended access path.\n- The image and manifest revisions can be traced to the approved release.\n\n## 11. Practical Exercise\n\nCreate a namespace and deploy a small application using a Deployment manifest. Add a Service that selects the application Pods. Inspect the Deployment, ReplicaSet, Pods, Service, and endpoints.\n\nChange the image reference to an invalid image and observe the resulting image-pull failure. Use kubectl describe and events to identify the reason. Restore the valid image reference and observe the successful rollout.\n\nTest the application through the Service. Explain the difference between a Pod being created, a container running, a Pod becoming ready, and the application successfully responding to a request.\n\n## 12. Interview and Engineering Understanding\n\nAn engineer should be able to explain what **Spring Boot Integration Testing — Assertions and Lifecycle** means, why Kubernetes provides the relevant capability, which API resources and cluster components participate, how reconciliation or execution works, and how the final state is verified.\n\nA strong practical answer describes the desired state, the actual state before and after the operation, the relevant manifest fields or commands, the evidence collected, and the production risks. Avoid simply stating that kubectl apply was successful. Explain whether the workload was scheduled, started, became ready, and served real application traffic."
